To improve the readability of the Bash script, here are some suggestions:

1. Use descriptive variable names and avoid using abbreviations or acronyms that may not be immediately understood by others. For example, instead of "master", use "CrawLab" to make it clear what the variable represents.

2. Comment your code to explain each part and how it interacts with one another. This helps to understand the logic and makes it easier to modify or adjust as needed.

3. Use whitespace effectively to separate blocks of code and to improve readability. For example, instead of having multiple lines of identical statements, try wrapping them in parentheses and separating them using commas or semicolons.

4. Use formatting to improve readability even further. Consider using tab characters or indentation to make the code easier to read and understand. You can also use whitespace to create vertical breaks within blocks of code to break up large sections and make it more readable.

5. Use meaningful variable names and keep them consistent throughout the script. This makes it easy to identify what each variable is used for and helps with maintainability.

6. Be specific when naming files or directories, making sure to use appropriate file extensions. For example, instead of "master", name the directory as "/root/.crawlab" to make it clear that this is a configuration directory rather than just a folder.

By following these suggestions and using consistent variable names and formatting throughout your code, you can improve readability and ensure that your Bash script is easy to understand and maintain.
